<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<populations>
  <!--
    Who actually turns up. A settlement of identical figures reads as a barracks,
    so arrivals roll from this table rather than from one blueprint. Every entry
    inherits r_KingdomSettler, where all settler behaviour lives, and overrides
    nothing but appearance - so another mod can put its own people on the road by
    merging one line here, and they will still house themselves, take work, and
    answer to the charter.
  -->
  <population Name="r_KingdomSettlers" Load="Merge">
    <object Blueprint="r_KingdomSettler" Number="1" Weight="40" />
    <object Blueprint="r_KingdomSettlerHand" Number="1" Weight="20" />
    <object Blueprint="r_KingdomSettlerDrifter" Number="1" Weight="14" />
    <object Blueprint="r_KingdomSettlerTinker" Number="1" Weight="10" />
    <object Blueprint="r_KingdomSettlerScribe" Number="1" Weight="8" />
    <object Blueprint="r_KingdomSettlerYoung" Number="1" Weight="8" />
  </population>
  <population Name="r_KingdomGuests" Load="Merge">
    <object Blueprint="r_KingdomGuest" Number="1" Weight="50" />
    <object Blueprint="r_KingdomGuestPilgrim" Number="1" Weight="25" />
    <object Blueprint="r_KingdomGuestNomad" Number="1" Weight="25" />
  </population>
  <population Name="r_KingdomNotableGuests" Load="Merge">
    <object Blueprint="r_KingdomNotableGuest" Number="1" Weight="50" />
    <object Blueprint="r_KingdomNotableGuestScribe" Number="1" Weight="25" />
    <object Blueprint="r_KingdomNotableGuestTinker" Number="1" Weight="25" />
  </population>
  <population Name="Tier1Wares" Load="Merge">
    <object Blueprint="r_FounderBasin" Number="1" Chance="25" />
    <object Blueprint="r_FoundingBook" Number="1" Chance="15" />
  </population>
  <population Name="Tier2Wares" Load="Merge">
    <object Blueprint="r_FounderBasin" Number="1" Chance="25" />
    <object Blueprint="r_FoundingBook" Number="1" Chance="15" />
  </population>
  <population Name="Tier1Wares" Load="Merge">
    <object Blueprint="r_KingdomCarrySign" Number="1" Chance="20" />
  </population>
  <population Name="Tier2Wares" Load="Merge">
    <object Blueprint="r_KingdomCarrySign" Number="1" Chance="20" />
  </population>
  <!--
    Seed, on the road. Addendum 11(b) asks that a farm start from real items the player obtains, so
    there has to be somewhere to obtain them that is not the farm itself. Merging into the tier
    wares tables is the honest answer: a trader who stocks small goods may be carrying seed, and
    which seed is the roll's business rather than the founder's. Chance rather than Weight, so
    these do not displace a merchant's own stock - each is asked independently and most rolls carry
    none of them.

    The low-tier crops are on the tier-1 table and the strange ones are not: elcatl spore-cake and
    dreadroot crowns turn up only where the wares are already tier 2, because a cake of spore that
    Qud has no colour word for is not something the first tinker on the road has in a sack. The
    other two ways to get seed - a harvest returning its own, and stripping a wild plant of the
    species - are in code and in ObjectBlueprints.xml respectively, and neither needs a table.
  -->
  <population Name="Tier1Wares" Load="Merge">
    <object Blueprint="r_KingdomSeedStarapple" Number="1" Chance="14" />
    <object Blueprint="r_KingdomSeedVinewafer" Number="1" Chance="14" />
    <object Blueprint="r_KingdomSeedMushroom" Number="1" Chance="10" />
  </population>
  <population Name="Tier2Wares" Load="Merge">
    <object Blueprint="r_KingdomSeedStarapple" Number="1" Chance="14" />
    <object Blueprint="r_KingdomSeedVinewafer" Number="1" Chance="14" />
    <object Blueprint="r_KingdomSeedMushroom" Number="1" Chance="12" />
    <object Blueprint="r_KingdomSeedGodshroom" Number="1" Chance="8" />
    <object Blueprint="r_KingdomSeedDreadroot" Number="1" Chance="8" />
  </population>
  <population Name="BookTent" Load="Merge">
    <object Blueprint="r_FoundingBook" Number="1" Chance="60" />
  </population>
  <!--
    A fifth entry here merged the book into DynamicObjectsTable:Books. It did nothing, and it was
    worse than nothing. That table is declared nowhere in the game: it is fabricated on demand from
    blueprints carrying a matching tag (XRL/PopulationManager.cs:189-211), no blueprint anywhere in
    StreamingAssets carries that tag, and no code rolls the table. Declaring the name in XML also
    pre-empts fabrication outright, because RequireTable returns early once the key exists - so if
    the tag ever did appear, this dead line would have suppressed the real table for the whole game.

    Tagging r_FoundingBook would be the correct mechanism rather than declaring the table, but it
    would still place the book nowhere, because nothing rolls that table in this build. The intent
    - the book turning up wherever books turn up - has no working mechanism here yet, and saying so
    is better than shipping a line that reads like it works. Art/check_xml_refs.py fails if it
    comes back.
  -->
  <!--
    Furnishings. The single-cell civic objects that used to be commissioned one at a time are now
    what a finished plot is furnished WITH, populated the way a vanilla hut is. The blueprints did
    not change and nothing was deleted; what changed is that a founder orders a house and gets the
    bunk, rather than ordering the bunk. Each table is a weighted single pick, rolled
    KingdomPlotRules.ContentsRolls(Size) times per finished plot.
  -->
  <population Name="r_KingdomFurnishings_Dwelling">
    <object Blueprint="r_KingdomBunk" Number="1" Weight="60" />
    <object Blueprint="Torchpost" Number="1" Weight="25" />
    <object Blueprint="r_KingdomBench" Number="1" Weight="15" />
  </population>
  <!--
    The water table furnishes with VESSELS and never with producers, and that is deliberate. A
    water design's Carries figure is derived from its own LiquidProducer (ObjectBlueprints.xml,
    the water works block), so a furnishing that also produced would put more in the casks than
    the ledger ever counted. Catchbasin is vanilla's own stoneware collector - the thing an Air
    Well deploys beside itself - with StartVolume="0", 64 drams, Collector="true"; anything on a
    finished plot carrying a LiquidVolume is dedicated to the settlement's stores by
    KingdomPlot2, so a basin is real capacity against the stage gate the moment it lands.

    Village Monument Amphora is the other obvious candidate and is deliberately NOT here: it
    ships StartVolume="0-32" InitialLiquid="water-1000" (B/ObjectBlueprints/Furniture.xml:5626),
    so every roll would arrive holding free fresh water. A vessel that arrives full is a
    producer wearing a vessel's name.
  -->
  <population Name="r_KingdomFurnishings_Water">
    <object Blueprint="r_KingdomCaskRack" Number="1" Weight="55" />
    <object Blueprint="Catchbasin" Number="1" Weight="30" />
    <object Blueprint="Torchpost" Number="1" Weight="15" />
  </population>
  <population Name="r_KingdomFurnishings_Civic">
    <object Blueprint="r_KingdomBench" Number="1" Weight="55" />
    <object Blueprint="Torchpost" Number="1" Weight="30" />
    <object Blueprint="Bookshelf" Number="1" Weight="15" />
  </population>
  <population Name="r_KingdomFurnishings_Comfort">
    <object Blueprint="Hookah" Number="1" Weight="40" />
    <object Blueprint="r_KingdomBench" Number="1" Weight="35" />
    <object Blueprint="Torchpost" Number="1" Weight="25" />
  </population>
  <population Name="r_KingdomFurnishings_Learning">
    <object Blueprint="Bookshelf" Number="1" Weight="60" />
    <object Blueprint="r_KingdomBench" Number="1" Weight="25" />
    <object Blueprint="Torchpost" Number="1" Weight="15" />
  </population>
</populations>
